    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "i'm not 100% sure on the bearing capacity stuff/moment capacity off the top of my head. i mainly just wanted to know what size timber i should be looking for at the lumber yard so it doesn't sag or anything. just assume standard structural grade douglas fir for now. is there anything else i should be looking out for in the wood itself that might mess with the load distribution?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "For a standard structural grade Douglas Fir, I recommend checking the NAFS (National Design Specification for Wooden Construction) or ASCE (American Society of Civil Engineers) standards for beam sizes. Based on the live and dead loads you provided, a beam size around 6-8 inches in diameter should be suitable. \n\nFor load distribution, keep an eye on the beam's grain orientation, as it can affect the shear capacity. Ideally, the grain should run perpendicular to the span, so consider the grain direction when selecting your timber. Additionally, avoid doing so over an area with knots or other defects, as they can create weak points in the beam's load path.",
